/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Start with a simple chord, maybe a major triad. Play it as an open chord \(the third is played an octave higher se there's more space in between notes\). Establish an ostinato or an arpeggio motion. After a few beats choose one of the 3 voices and move it up or down by a half step. The resulting chords don't have to be known chords, good voice-leading allow for bizarre chords to make sense. See the example below:)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 131 0 R
/Pg 15 0 R
/A [605 0 R 0]
/K [4]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Overall the progression above is quite unusual, surprising, profound, beautifully ambiguous. PRACTICE: Create a few series of voice-led harmonies without a melody in mind, just playing a chord and slowly transforming it. Get familiar with the different possibilities and sounds you can obtain. Then try applying is to simple melodies. If you hit a wall, you may interrupt the voice leading as a last resort and shift the whole chord, although I believe there's ALWAYS a solution half-step away.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (Another great exercise is to try to play a known tune, for example My Funny Valentine, and accompany using this principle, starting with an open chord and moving only one note at a time. You'll have to think creatively...)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
