265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 608 0 R
/E (Sing everything you play, your voice is the best link of communication between your heart and your fingers. Singing is the best way to make your music feel lyrical, organic and natural. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 609 0 R
/E (True technique is measured by how soft one can play, not by how fast. True instrumental control ris the ability to play softly and precisely. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 610 0 R
/E (Practice imitating your favorite musicians, don't be afraid of mimicking everything they play. Try to imitate a famous musician and have your friends guess who you are imitating. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 611 0 R
/E (Learn as many different genres as you can: classical, early jazz, funk, latin, Balkan, African, pop. The more the better. The best musicians are often musical polyglots. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 612 0 R
/E (Memorize as much music as possible. Force yourself to rely on your ears, avoiding sheet music-dependency. It's hard in the beginning, but it gets easier as you learn how your memory works, and what types of information you need in order to memorize. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 613 0 R
/E (Practice transposing melodies to different keys \(and harmonies too\), but instead of doing any guesses, take your time, use your ears and your theory to not make mistakes. Don't do it by trial and error, practice developing confidence and accuracy. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 614 0 R
/E (Use creative constraints in your practice. Too much freedom often leads to paralysis. Establish a few rules to your improvisation practice: using a specific set of notes, or only one type of rhythmic motif, or only upbeats, etc.  )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
